From: SwiftPili <359733720+haikiseven@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] input: seek slaves to the requested time, not the master's landing

SlaveSeek() asked each slave to seek to DEMUX_GET_TIME of the master. For a
fragmented stream that reports where the master LANDED -- the start of the
fragment containing the request -- and not the request itself. That is wrong
twice over.

First, the slave lands on the fragment containing the master's fragment start.
Whenever the two streams use different fragment durations, that is a whole
fragment earlier than the master. DASH is the ordinary case: BiliBili's video
fragments are exactly 5.000s while its audio fragments are AAC-frame aligned at
5.01551s, so video boundaries 5.000n always fall just below audio boundaries
5.01551n and the audio slave lands ~5s early for every n >= 1:

  [seek to 22.5]
  seeking to sidx moof pos ... 20000000     <- video lands at 20.000
  seeking to sidx moof pos ...  15046530    <- audio lands at 15.047

Audio is the clock master, so the video output holds its last frame while the
clock climbs the gap, and libVLC eventually resynchronises by discarding the
audio it just played ("losing 5015304 us of audio").

Second, and worse: an accurate DEMUX_SET_TIME makes the mp4 demuxer publish its
target as the es_out preroll point (ES_OUT_SET_NEXT_DISPLAY_TIME). That point is
a single global (es_out_sys_t::i_preroll_end), and the slaves are seeked after
the master, so the slave OVERWRITES the master's preroll with the master's own
landing time. EsOutDecodersStopBuffering() then computes

    i_preroll_duration = max(i_preroll_end - i_stream_start, 0)

as zero. Nothing is ever marked BLOCK_FLAG_PREROLL. Instead of discarding the
run-up at full speed during the buffering phase, playback resumes at the fragment
boundary and crawls to the requested time at 1x -- up to one fragment duration of
dead time on every seek, on every file, whether or not any slave landed early.
Observed on a stream with 10s fragments: a seek to 83.3s resumed at 80.08s and
took 3.2s of wall clock to arrive, with "Stream buffering done (5000 ms ...)"
showing the buffering target was just the network caching, with no preroll term.

Seek the slaves to the requested time instead. They then land no earlier than the
master, and every demuxer publishes the same preroll point.

When the request is unreachable for a slave -- it may be shorter than the master,
and a seek past its end fails -- fall back to the master's landing time, which is
what we used to pass unconditionally. Without that fallback a short slave would
be marked b_eof and disabled for the rest of playback.

Only ControlSetTime knows the requested time. ControlSetPosition has a position
rather than a time, and recovering one would depend on how `val` was normalised
against the master's length; it keeps the old DEMUX_GET_TIME behaviour, as does
the seekpoint path.

Generated with --function-context: ControlSetTime and ControlSetPosition end in
byte-identical blocks, so a 3-line-context hunk matches either one and `git apply`
will silently place them by proximity -- swapping them if the target file's line
numbers differ from the one the diff was generated against.

Generated with 12 lines of context. ControlSetTime and ControlSetPosition end in
byte-identical blocks:

        if( i_ret == VLC_SUCCESS )
        {
            if( priv->i_slave > 0 )
                SlaveSeek( p_input );
            priv->master->b_eof = false;
        }
        return i_ret;
    }

so a 3-line-context hunk matches either, and `git apply` places it by proximity.
A diff generated against a tree whose line numbers differ therefore SWAPS the two
hunks -- silently, and the result still compiles. Twelve lines of context reach
each function's unique body, so the hunks cannot float; on a tree they no longer
fit, the patch fails to apply instead of misapplying.

Verify placement after applying (build-libvlc.sh treats a failed patch as
non-fatal): patches/libvlc/verify/check-applied.sh

